Q1. Introduce your project.
Answer

My project is an AI-based Drowsiness Detection System that monitors a user's eye state through a webcam and plays an alarm whenever it detects that the user's eyes have remained closed for a certain duration.

The application captures live video using OpenCV. Every frame from the webcam is processed and passed to MediaPipe Face Landmarker, which detects facial landmarks in real time. From these landmarks, I extract only the eye-related points and calculate whether the eyes are open or closed.

Instead of triggering an alarm whenever a single frame detects closed eyes, I use a timer. If the eyes remain closed continuously for more than the threshold duration, the system considers the user drowsy and plays an alarm using Pygame.

The overall pipeline is:

Webcam → OpenCV → MediaPipe Face Landmarker → Eye Landmark Analysis → Drowsiness Detection → Alarm

The project runs completely on the local machine, requires no internet connection, and performs inference in real time.

Interviewer's Follow-up

Q: Why did you choose this project?

Answer

Because it combines Computer Vision, AI, real-time processing, optimization, and practical problem-solving. It also gave me experience integrating multiple libraries into one complete application rather than training a model from scratch.

Q2. Explain the complete workflow of your project from start to finish.
Answer

The workflow consists of several stages.

OpenCV initializes the webcam.
Each captured frame is converted into the format expected by MediaPipe.
MediaPipe first detects the face.
After detecting the face, it predicts facial landmarks.
From all detected landmarks, I select only those corresponding to the left and right eyes.
I calculate whether the eyes are open or closed.
If both eyes remain closed continuously beyond a predefined duration, the system classifies the user as drowsy.
Pygame plays an alarm.
Once the eyes open again, the alarm stops and the timer resets.

This entire cycle repeats for every frame captured from the webcam.

Interviewer's Follow-up

Why don't you directly play the alarm when eyes close?

Because people blink naturally. A blink lasts around 100–400 milliseconds. Triggering an alarm immediately would create many false alarms. Using a timer differentiates normal blinking from actual drowsiness.

Q3. Why did you use OpenCV?
Answer

OpenCV acts as the interface between my application and the webcam.

Its primary responsibilities are:

Capturing live video.
Reading frames continuously.
Displaying processed frames.
Drawing annotations like eye landmarks and status text.
Handling keyboard input.
Performing basic image processing.

OpenCV does not detect drowsiness by itself.

Instead, it provides image frames to MediaPipe, which performs AI inference.

I chose OpenCV because it is lightweight, optimized for real-time computer vision, and has excellent Python support.

Interviewer's Follow-up

Can OpenCV detect faces?

Yes.

OpenCV provides Haar Cascade and DNN-based detectors.

However, for this project I preferred MediaPipe because it provides not only the face but also highly accurate facial landmarks.

Q4. What exactly does OpenCV do internally when reading webcam frames?
Answer

When OpenCV opens the webcam using VideoCapture(0), it creates a connection with the camera driver.

Each iteration:

Camera captures an image.
The image is transferred into memory.
OpenCV converts it into a NumPy array.
The array contains pixel values in BGR format.
My program receives this frame.

So OpenCV itself does not "understand" the image.

It simply transfers pixel data from the camera to my program.

Those pixels are then analyzed by MediaPipe.

Interviewer's Follow-up

Why is every frame stored as a NumPy array?

Because NumPy provides efficient matrix operations.

Since images are matrices of pixels, NumPy allows fast access and modification.

Q5. Why did you use MediaPipe instead of OpenCV Haar Cascade?
Answer

Haar Cascade only detects whether a face exists.

It cannot accurately identify individual eye landmarks.

My project requires precise eye positions.

MediaPipe provides:

Face detection
Face landmarks
Eye landmarks
Face tracking
High accuracy
Real-time performance

Another advantage is that MediaPipe continues tracking landmarks even if the face moves slightly, making it much more stable than Haar Cascade.

Therefore, MediaPipe was the better choice.

Interviewer's Follow-up

Is Haar Cascade AI?

No.

Haar Cascade is a machine learning classifier trained using positive and negative images.

It is not a deep learning model.

MediaPipe uses deep learning models.

Q6. Why not use YOLO for this project?
Answer

YOLO is primarily an object detection model.

It can detect a face but cannot directly provide detailed eye landmarks.

To calculate whether eyes are open or closed, I need precise landmark positions.

MediaPipe already provides these landmarks efficiently.

YOLO would also increase computational cost unnecessarily for this application.

Therefore, MediaPipe is more suitable.

Interviewer's Follow-up

Can YOLO detect eyes?

Yes.

If trained for eye detection.

But it still won't provide the detailed landmark coordinates needed for eye-state analysis.

Q7. What is MediaPipe?
Answer

MediaPipe is an open-source framework developed by Google for building real-time perception pipelines.

It provides pre-trained models for tasks like:

Face Detection
Face Mesh
Hand Tracking
Pose Estimation
Object Detection

Instead of training models ourselves, we can use these optimized pipelines directly.

MediaPipe is highly optimized for CPU execution, making it suitable for laptops without dedicated GPUs.

Interviewer's Follow-up

Why is MediaPipe fast?

Because:

Lightweight neural networks
Optimized inference
Landmark tracking between frames
Reduced repeated detection
Q8. Which model is actually used inside MediaPipe Face Landmarker?
Answer

MediaPipe Face Landmarker internally combines multiple models.

The first stage uses BlazeFace, a lightweight face detector.

Once the face is detected, another neural network predicts hundreds of facial landmarks across the face.

Instead of detecting the face from scratch in every frame, MediaPipe tracks the face across consecutive frames, improving speed.

This pipeline allows real-time performance while maintaining high accuracy.

Interviewer's Follow-up

Did you train BlazeFace?

No.

It is a pre-trained model provided by MediaPipe.

Q9. What is BlazeFace?
Answer

BlazeFace is Google's lightweight deep learning face detection model.

It was specifically designed for:

Mobile phones
Real-time applications
Low latency
High FPS

Unlike heavier detectors, BlazeFace balances speed and accuracy.

It detects:

Face bounding box
Initial facial region

That output is then passed to the landmark model.

Interviewer's Follow-up

Why not use ResNet?

ResNet is much larger.

It provides higher capacity but requires significantly more computation.

BlazeFace is designed specifically for real-time face detection.

Q10. After BlazeFace detects the face, what happens next?
Answer

The detected face region is cropped internally.

This cropped face is passed into the facial landmark model.

The landmark model predicts hundreds of key facial points.

Each point has:

x coordinate
y coordinate
z depth

These landmarks correspond to:

Eyes
Nose
Mouth
Lips
Eyebrows
Jawline

My project extracts only the eye-related landmarks.

Interviewer's Follow-up

Does BlazeFace detect eyes?

No.

BlazeFace detects only the face.

The landmark model predicts eye locations.

Q11. How does MediaPipe know which points belong to the eyes?
Answer

Each facial landmark has a predefined index.

For example:

Landmark 33 always corresponds to a specific point near the left eye.
Landmark 263 corresponds to the right eye.

MediaPipe always outputs landmarks in the same order.

Therefore, by using predefined eye landmark indices, I can isolate only the eye region.

I don't search for the eyes manually.

The model already labels them consistently.

Q12. Why are only eye landmarks extracted instead of processing the whole face?
Answer

The objective of my project is to detect drowsiness based on eye closure.

The rest of the face—such as the nose, lips, or jaw—does not directly contribute to this decision.

Processing only the required landmarks reduces unnecessary computations and simplifies the logic.

It also makes the code easier to maintain and improves readability.

Q13. What information does each landmark contain?
Answer

Each landmark contains:

x coordinate
y coordinate
z coordinate

The x and y coordinates represent the position on the image.

The z coordinate provides an approximate relative depth from the camera.

Together, these values describe the three-dimensional position of the facial point.

In my project, I mainly use the x and y coordinates because eye-state detection depends on the distance between points in the image plane.

Q14. Why is RGB conversion required before passing the frame to MediaPipe?
Answer

OpenCV reads images in BGR format by default.

However, MediaPipe models are trained to expect RGB images.

If a BGR image is passed directly, the colour channels are interpreted incorrectly, which can reduce detection accuracy.

Therefore, I convert the frame from BGR to RGB before inference.

After MediaPipe finishes processing, I convert it back to BGR for display using OpenCV.

Q15. What would happen if you skipped the RGB conversion?
Answer

The model would still receive an image, but the colour information would be incorrect because the blue and red channels would be swapped.

This may lead to poorer face detection, inaccurate landmarks, or unstable tracking depending on lighting conditions.

Although the image might still appear somewhat correct to a human, the model's input would not match the format it was trained on.

Q16. Why don't you process every alternate frame to improve speed?
Answer

That is a possible optimisation, but it comes with a trade-off.

Skipping frames reduces CPU usage and increases FPS, but it also increases the chance of missing short eye-closure events or introducing latency in detection.

For a drowsiness detection system, continuous monitoring provides better reliability, so I chose to process every frame.

Q17. How do you avoid false alarms caused by blinking?
Answer

A normal blink lasts only a fraction of a second. If the alarm were triggered based on a single frame, almost every blink would be treated as drowsiness.

To solve this, I measure how long the eyes remain closed. Only when the closed-eye state continues beyond a predefined threshold do I classify it as drowsiness and activate the alarm.

This time-based approach greatly reduces false positives.

Q18. What are false positives and false negatives in your project?
Answer

A false positive occurs when the system incorrectly identifies an alert person as drowsy. For example, a long blink or poor lighting might briefly make the eyes appear closed.

A false negative occurs when the system fails to detect actual drowsiness. This might happen if the face is partially occluded, the user is wearing certain types of glasses, or the camera angle is poor.

The goal is to minimise both, although improving one often affects the other, so a balance is required.

Q19. What limitations does your project have?
Answer

Some limitations include:

Performance may decrease in very low-light environments.
Extreme head rotations can reduce landmark accuracy.
Certain sunglasses or heavy eye occlusions may hide the eyes.
The system currently analyses only one primary face effectively.
Drowsiness is determined mainly from eye closure and does not consider yawning, head nodding, or physiological signals.

These limitations could be addressed in future versions by combining additional cues and models.

